  "style": {
    "id": "photoelastic-stress",
    "name": "Photoelastic Stress",
    "description": "Stress shows as colour: a clear notched plastic beam in an aluminium loading frame stands in front of a bright upright light box; a second polarising sheet slides across and, under it, the beam turns black and blooms with interference-colour bands (isochromatics of a dark-field circular polariscope) when the screw press loads it. Band colour is computed per pixel from the retardation R = Cf (σ1 − σ2) through a colour table integrated over the visible spectrum (blackbody light box × CIE colour-matching functions × sin²(πR/λ)); the stress is an analytical approximation (Flamant point loads for the press and every support + beam bending from the support reactions + a notch concentration factor). Bands crowd at the notch and the contacts and spread out when a third support shares the load.",
